    <h2>Why Do Clothing Manufacturers Ignore Most Inquiry Emails?</h2>
    <p>Reputable clothing factories receive 30–50 inquiry emails every day. They do not have time to respond to emails that require them to ask basic follow-up questions. If your inquiry does not immediately communicate that you are a serious, prepared buyer with a real order, it gets deleted — not out of rudeness, but out of commercial necessity.</p>
    <p>Research from sourcing professionals confirms that the emails most likely to be ignored share common characteristics:</p>

    <table class="rf-table" aria-label="Common email mistakes and fixes">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>❌ Mistake that gets you ignored</th>
          <th>✓ What this generator does instead</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"How much does it cost to make a t-shirt?"</td><td>Specifies garment type, fabric, quantity, and construction type</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 paragraphs about your brand vision</td><td>Leads with order specs — exactly what factories need to quote</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No quantity mentioned</td><td>States quantity per style clearly in the first block</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No timeline given</td><td>Specifies required delivery window and incoterms</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Asking "Can you make sustainable clothes?"</td><td>Names specific certifications (GOTS, OEKO-TEX, BSCI) required</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Asking the factory to design everything</td><td>Clearly states service type (OEM/ODM/CMT/Private Label)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What Is the Difference Between OEM, ODM, CMT, and Private Label?</h2>
    <p>Using the wrong term in your inquiry immediately signals inexperience. Here is what each means and when to use it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">OEM (Original Equipment Manufacturer)</strong> — You provide complete tech packs; the factory manufactures to your exact specifications. Full design control. You need a tech pack before contacting a factory for OEM</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">ODM (Original Design Manufacturer)</strong> — The factory has existing base designs and patterns that you can modify and brand. Faster, lower development cost, no tech pack required upfront</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">CMT (Cut, Make, Trim)</strong> — You source and ship fabric to the factory; they cut, sew, and finish only. Lower CM cost but you bear fabric sourcing and quality risk</li>
      <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Private Label</strong> — The factory has ready-made garments you put your brand label on. Fastest route to market, least customisation. Best for testing demand before investing in custom design</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Is FOB — And What Incoterms Should You Request?</h2>
    <p>When you contact a Bangladesh factory, they will almost always quote in <strong style="color:#fff;">FOB (Free on Board)</strong> — the price of the garment up to the point it is loaded onto a vessel at Chittagong port. You pay for freight, insurance, and import duty after that. FOB is the safest default for buyers who have their own freight forwarder.</p>
    <p>If you want the factory to arrange freight to your destination port, request <strong style="color:#fff;">CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight)</strong>. If you want complete door-to-door delivery including duty, request <strong style="color:#fff;">DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)</strong> — though this is less common with Bangladesh manufacturers. Use our <a href="/tools/garment-cost-calculator" style="color:#e00;" title="Garment cost calculator">Garment Cost Calculator</a> to see the full landed cost under each scenario.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07);padding-bottom:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Do I need to pay for samples before production?</h3>
      <p>Yes, most factories charge a sample fee of $50–$250 per style depending on complexity. This is credited against your bulk order deposit when you confirm production. Some ODM and private label factories offer free samples to serious buyers — SDF includes sample cost details in our quote response.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07);padding-bottom:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Should I contact multiple factories at once?</h3>
      <p>Yes — most experienced sourcing professionals contact 3–5 factories simultaneously for any new style to compare pricing, lead times, and responsiveness. Your generated email is professional enough to send to multiple factories without editing. SDF responds within 48 hours to all structured inquiries.</p>
